

-> What are Design Patterns?
Design Patterns are proven, reusable solutions to common software design problems.
They are:
    NOT code
    NOT frameworks
    Blueprints / best practices

Example:
    “How to create only ONE object?” → Singleton
    “How to create objects without tight coupling?” → Factory

-> Why are Design Patterns used?
    To solve recurring problems
    To write clean, maintainable code
    To follow OOP principles
    To make code flexible and scalable

-> Advantages of Design Patterns
   ✔ Reusable solutions
   ✔ Improves code readability
   ✔ Reduces tight coupling
   ✔ Improves maintainability
   ✔ Interview-friendly (very important)

-> Design Patterns vs Frameworks
| Design Pattern       | Framework         |
| -------------------- | ----------------- |
| Concept / Idea       | Ready-made code   |
| Language independent | Language specific |
| You implement it     | You use it        |
| Example: Singleton   | Example: Spring   |


-> Real-Life Example
        Factory Pattern → Pizza shop deciding which pizza to make
        Singleton → Government (only one)
        Observer → YouTube notifications
        Strategy → Different payment methods